==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|assiduous|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In an [[assiduous]] manner; [[diligently]], [[industriously]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1898|author=H.G. Wells|authorlink=H.G. Wells|title={{w|The War of the Worlds}}|publisher=William Heinemann|location=London|page=93|passage=They were all too '''assiduously''' engaged to talk to us as we passed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=12
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=There were many wooden chairs for the bulk of his visitors, and two wicker armchairs with red cloth cushions for superior people. From the packing-cases had emerged some Indian clubs,&nbsp;[…], and all these articles&nbsp;[…] made a scattered and untidy decoration that Mrs. Clough '''assiduously''' dusted and greatly cherished.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1938|author=Norman Lindsay|authorlink=Norman Lindsay|title=Age of Consent|publisher=Ure Smith|location=Sydney|year_published=1962|page=231|passage=Her voice caught in her throat and another violent shiver ran through her. Bradly thought she was going to cry and became actively disturbed, holding her with one arm and patting her shoulder '''assiduously'''. "Damme, don't think about it, Cora.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2003|title=Matrix Reloaded|role=The Architect|passage=While it remains a burden '''assiduously''' avoided, it is not unexpected, and thus not beyond a measure of control. Which has led you, inexorably, here.}}
#* '''2020''', Frank Bowman, The role of the chief justice in an impeachment trial, SCOTUSblog (Jan. 10, 2020, 11:18 AM), [https://www.scotusblog.com/2020/01/the-role-of-the-chief-justice-in-an-impeachment-trial/ online]
#*: What little precedent we have in such cases suggests that chief justices keep their heads down and, so far as possible, defer '''assiduously''' to the will of the Senate majority.

